# Migrating to Basin 2.0

This guide covers the changes needed to move existing Basin 1.x code to 2.0.
Start with your dependency features and problem definitions, then update state
construction, stopping conditions, and result handling. Check the changed solver
defaults before comparing numerical results.

The [API reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/) documents full signatures
and settings. If you have saved runs, read [checkpoint files](#checkpoint-files)
before upgrading.

<h2 id="backend-features">Update your dependency features</h2>

<span id="test-problems"></span>

Basin 2.0 enables no features by default. If you import `basin::problems`, add
`problems` explicitly, keeping any other features you already use:

```toml
[dependencies]
basin = { version = "2", features = ["problems"] }
```

The unversioned backend features now follow the newest supported release.
`nalgebra` and `nalgebra-lapack` move from nalgebra 0.34 to 0.35, which requires
Rust 1.89. To keep nalgebra 0.34, use `nalgebra_v0_34` and
`nalgebra_v0_34-lapack` instead. The package MSRV remains Rust 1.87 for features
that do not select nalgebra 0.35.

<h2 id="declaring-constraints">Declare constraints on every problem</h2>

Every `CostFunction` and `Residual` implementation now also needs
`ProblemConstraints`. For an unconstrained problem, add:

```rust
impl basin::ProblemConstraints for MyProblem {
    type Constraints = basin::NoConstraints;

    fn constraint_set(&self) -> basin::NoConstraints {
        basin::NoConstraints::EMPTY
    }
}
```

For a constrained problem, replace the old constraint traits with one typed
`Constraints` set. You can keep bounds and matrices in their existing fields
and borrow them from `constraint_set`. A box-only problem, for example, becomes:

```rust
use basin::{BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, Constraints, ProblemConstraints};

struct BoxProblem {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl ProblemConstraints for BoxProblem {
    type Constraints = Constraints<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraint_set(
        &self,
    ) -> <Self::Constraints as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        Constraints::new()
            .with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(Some(&self.lower), Some(&self.upper)))
    }
}
```

Build the set from the blocks your problem has:

| Removed 1.x trait | 2.0 replacement |
| --- | --- |
| `BoxConstraints` | `.with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(lower, upper))` |
| `LinearEqualityConstraints` | `.with_linear_equalities(LinearBlock::new(matrix, rhs))` |
| `LinearInequalityConstraints` | `.with_linear_inequalities(LinearBlock::new(matrix, rhs))` |
| `LinearConstraints` | Put the bounds and linear blocks in the same set. |
| `NonlinearInequalityConstraints` | `.with_nonlinear_inequalities(count)` and `NonlinearInequalityValues` |
| `NonlinearConstraints` | Declare the nonlinear blocks and implement `NonlinearEqualityValues` and/or `NonlinearInequalityValues`. |

Declare the complete constraint set. A solver must support every declared kind;
a box-only solver cannot accept a problem that also declares nonlinear
constraints. Remove `FoldedConstraints::new(problem)` when using COBYLA and pass
the declared problem directly.

Nonlinear value traits now declare their own `Param`, `Values`, and `Error`
types. Their callbacks return `Result<Values, ConstraintSolveError<Error>>`.
`ConstraintJacobian` likewise declares `Param`, `Jacobian`, and `Error` and
returns the wrapped error type. Its rows contain nonlinear equalities first,
then nonlinear inequalities; exclude linear rows. Equalities use `h(x) = 0`,
and inequalities use `c(x) <= 0`.

Constrained solves also return `ConstraintSolveError<E>`. Update error handling
to distinguish `Evaluation(E)` from `InvalidConstraints(ConstraintError)`.
Existing callback errors can be wrapped with
`map_err(ConstraintSolveError::Evaluation)`.

<h3 id="sampling-domains-and-local-relaxations">Update hybrid solver adapters</h3>

MA-LS now requires sampling bounds on an unconstrained objective. Wrap an
existing box-only problem in `SamplingDomain::new(problem)` to keep using its
box for sampling while allowing local chains to move outside it.

For bounded CMA or DE injection with an unconstrained local solver, wrap that
solver in `RelaxedInner`:

```rust
let solver = basin::DeInject::with_inner_solver(
    basin::De::new(42),
    basin::RelaxedInner::new(basin::NelderMead::new()),
);
```

A local solver that enforces the same box, such as bounded L-BFGS, can still be
passed directly.

<h2 id="shared-progress-states">Replace algorithm-specific states</h2>

Solvers now own their models, history, random generators, and workspace. Shared
states hold evaluated points, costs, and progress. If your code uses
`Executor::from_start`, it already lets the solver choose the state:

```rust
let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, x0)
    .max_iter(1_000)
    .run()?;
```

If you construct states explicitly, replace the old types as follows. Generic
parameters are omitted here for readability; shared vector states use
`<V, F = f64>`.

| Basin 1.x state | Basin 2.0 state |
| --- | --- |
| `BasicState` for gradient descent, projected gradient descent, or trust regions | `FirstOrderState` |
| `BasicState` for SGD, basin hopping, or the barrier method | `PointState` |
| `BasicState` for the augmented-Lagrangian method | `SelectedState` |
| `QuasiNewtonState` and its backend aliases, `LbfgsState` | `FirstOrderState` |
| `NllsState` | `PointState` |
| `BasicSimplexState` | `SimplexProgress` |
| `BasicPopulationState`, `CmaEsState`, `GlobalBestPsoState` | `PopulationProgress` |
| `MaLsChGenericState`, `MaLsChState`, `MaLsChSwState` | `PopulationProgress` |
| `NewuoaState`, `BobyqaState`, `LincoaState`, `GbnmState`, `SolisWetsState` | `PointState` |
| `MadsState` | `PointState` |
| `ConstrainedMadsState`, `CobylaState` | `SelectedState` |
| `SlsqpState` | `SelectedFirstOrderState` |
| `SimulatedAnnealingState` | `ProposalState` |
| `ScalarState<F>` | `PointState<F, F>` |
| `ScalarGradientState<F>` | `FirstOrderState<F, F>` |

Point-like states use `new(x)`. For Nelder-Mead, use
`SimplexProgress::new(x)` or `SimplexProgress::from_simplex(vertices)`. Random
search, DE, SSGA, PSO, and MA-LS take `PopulationProgress::empty()` to sample a
population or `from_population(members)` to use explicit members. Construction
does not evaluate a point: checked `current()` and `best()` accessors return
`None` until a record is available.

Several initialization settings moved from state to solver. Carry these over
when replacing the state constructor:

- **L-BFGS:** replace `LbfgsState::new(x, m)` with `FirstOrderState::new(x)`
  and set `solver.with_m_capacity(m)`.
- **CMA-ES:** replace `CmaEsState::new(mean, sigma).with_stds(stds)` with
  `PopulationProgress::from_point(mean)` and
  `CmaEs::new(seed, sigma).with_stds(stds)`. Apply the same change to bounded CMA.
  Specify the matrix type on the solver if it was previously inferred from state.
- **Solis-Wets:** replace `SolisWetsState::new(x, rho)` with
  `PointState::new(x)` and `SolisWets::new(seed).with_initial_step_size(rho)`.
- **Global-best PSO:** replace `from_positions_and_velocities(xs, vs)` with
  `PopulationProgress::from_population(xs)` and
  `solver.with_initial_velocities(Some(vs))`.
- **Population size:** replace `BasicPopulationState::with_size(n)` with
  `PopulationProgress::empty()`. Set the size through `RandomSearch::new(n, seed)`
  or `with_pop_size(n)` on DE and SSGA.
- **BFGS:** configure a custom initial inverse Hessian through
  `solver.with_initial_inverse_hessian(matrix)` rather than state.

For these solvers, reusing progress starts a fresh run and resets the model,
history, and RNG. Population searches reuse and reevaluate supplied members;
CMA rebuilds its distribution around the reported point. To continue the same
trajectory, retain both solver and state; see [checkpoint files](#checkpoint-files).

<h3 id="observing-solver-diagnostics">Read algorithm diagnostics from the solver</h3>

Move calls for algorithm quantities from state to solver. This includes BFGS's
`inverse_hessian()`, CMA's `mean()` and `sigma()`, annealing's `temperature()`,
PSO's velocities and personal bests, MADS's mesh settings, interpolation solvers'
`rho()`, and SLSQP's stationarity and multipliers. Some getters now return
`Option` because the model is unavailable before initialization.

Use `run_with_solver()` when you need the final solver, or
`stepper.solver()` between steps. To migrate an observer that previously read
these quantities from state, use `observe_solver`:

```rust
use basin::{ObserverMode, State};

executor.observe_solver(
    |state, solver, event| {
        println!("{event:?}: {} {}", state.iter(), solver.temperature());
    },
    ObserverMode::Every(10),
).run()?;
```

This example assumes an annealing executor. Progress-only observers can keep
using `Observe<S>`.

<h3 id="constraint-violation-reports">Update readers of evaluated progress</h3>

Constrained states return a `ViolationReport` alongside the point and objective
in `current()` and `best()`. Read `report.value` for the numeric violation.
The measure remains solver-specific: squared L2 for constrained MADS, L1 for
SLSQP, maximum violation for COBYLA, and L2 for the augmented-Lagrangian method.

Population costs are no longer guaranteed to be sorted. Bounded CMA now reports
clipped points with raw objective costs; read `genotypes()` and
`penalized_costs()` from the solver if you need its internal ranking.

SGD now returns its last evaluated point with its matching cost. If costs are
refreshed less often than every step, this can lag behind the working iterate.
Use `with_cost_eval_every(1)` to report every iterate, or read
`working_param()` from the retained solver.

<h2 id="stopping-conditions">Move stopping conditions to their owners</h2>

`TerminationCriterion`, its built-in criterion types, and `terminate_on` are
removed. Put numerical tolerances on the solver and execution limits on the
executor:

```rust
let solver = GradientDescent::new(0.1)
    .with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, x0)
    .max_iter(1_000)
    .max_cost_evals(10_000)
    .run()?;
```

Use this mapping for existing criteria. The same split applies to
`InnerExecutor`; composed solvers' `inner_terminate_on` methods are also removed.

| Removed criterion | Replacement |
| --- | --- |
| `MaxIter`, `MaxCostEvals`, `MaxGradientEvals`, `MaxTime` | Executor: `max_iter`, `max_cost_evals`, `max_gradient_evals`, `max_time` |
| `TargetCost`, `NoImprovement`, `NoAcceptance` | Executor: `target_objective`, `no_objective_improvement`, `no_acceptance` |
| `GradientTolerance`, `RelativeGradientTolerance` | Solver: `with_absolute_gradient_tolerance`, `with_relative_gradient_tolerance` |
| `ProjectedGradientTolerance` | `with_absolute_projected_gradient_tolerance`; bounds come from the problem. |
| `ParamTolerance`, `RelativeParamTolerance` | `with_absolute_step_tolerance`, `with_relative_step_tolerance` |
| `CostTolerance`, `RelativeCostTolerance` | `with_absolute_cost_change_tolerance`, `with_relative_cost_change_tolerance` |
| `SimplexTolerance` | `with_absolute_simplex_size_tolerance` and `with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance` |
| `CmaEsTolerance` | `with_absolute_distribution_size_tolerance` |
| `RhoTolerance` | `with_absolute_radius_tolerance`, or `with_absolute_step_size_tolerance` for Solis-Wets |
| `MeshTolerance` | `with_absolute_poll_size_tolerance` |

Optional tolerances use `None` to disable a check; zero now means an exact-zero
threshold. Repeating a setter replaces that setting. Distinct numerical tests
usually combine with OR, while Nelder-Mead's enabled simplex size and cost-spread
tests combine with AND. Check the solver's documented norm and scaling when
replacing a generic criterion.

`target_cost` and `no_improvement` are renamed to `target_objective` and
`no_objective_improvement`. They require an objective-ordered incumbent.
`SelectedState` and `SelectedFirstOrderState` do not provide that guarantee:
their solvers may select a higher-cost point as feasibility improves. For those
states, use an application stop that checks both violation and objective.

Replace custom criteria with `stop_when`, returning `Some(ApplicationStop)` to
stop or `None` to continue:

```rust
executor.stop_when(|state| {
    (state.iter() >= 500).then(|| basin::ApplicationStop::new("user_requested"))
})
```

For reusable inner solves with captured history, use `stop_when_factory` to
create a fresh closure for each run. Composed solvers expose
`inner_stop_when_factory`.

Execution controls now run in a fixed order before numerical convergence,
regardless of the old criterion registration order. Budgets are checked after
initialization and between steps, so one step can exceed an evaluation budget.

<h3 id="evaluation-counters">Update evaluation budgets</h3>

Shared states' `cost_evals()` and `gradient_evals()` now count only their named
callback categories. They no longer fold other evaluations into those totals.
This matters especially for least squares, which typically makes no cost calls:
replace `max_cost_evals(n)` with
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::Residual, n)`.

Nonlinear constraint callbacks count as residual evaluations, and constraint
Jacobians count as Jacobian evaluations.

Use `state.counts()` or `result.counts` for all categories. Budget Hessians,
Jacobian calls, and inner-solver derivative work explicitly with
`max_evaluations(kind, n)`, or use `EvaluationKind::TotalWork` for their sum,
including objective calls. On states without gradients, including SGD's new
`PointState`, replace `max_gradient_evals(n)` with
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::Gradient, n)`.

<h2 id="structured-termination-reports">Read the termination report</h2>

Replace `result.reason` with `result.report.termination` and matches on
`TerminationReason` with matches on `Termination`:

```rust
match &result.report.termination {
    basin::Termination::Converged(_) => println!("converged"),
    basin::Termination::Limit(limits) => println!("budget exhausted: {limits:?}"),
    stop => println!("stopped: {stop:?}"),
}
```

A numerical failure is reported through `Termination::Failed` with a returned
state; callback errors still return `Err`. Code that treats every `Ok` result
as convergence should inspect the termination instead.

Update observers and steppers to receive the report:

| Basin 1.x | Basin 2.0 |
| --- | --- |
| `Observe::observe_final(..., &TerminationReason)` | `observe_final(..., &TerminationReport<S::Float>)` |
| `ObservationEvent::Final(reason)` | `ObservationEvent::Final(&report)` |
| `StepOutcome::Stopped(reason)` | `StepOutcome::Stopped(report)` |
| `Stepper::finished() -> Option<TerminationReason>` | `Option<&TerminationReport<S::Float>>` |
| `reason as u8` | `result.report.code().as_u8()` |

Final observer events borrow the report; clone it if you need to retain it.
`StepOutcome` is now `Clone` rather than `Copy`. The numeric termination code
preserves the 1.x identifiers but cannot represent every detail of the report.

<h2 id="solver-and-line-search-settings">Update solver names and settings</h2>

<h3 id="bounded-least-squares-solver-name">Rename Trf</h3>

Rename `Trf<V, M, F>` to `BoundedLevenbergMarquardt<V, M, F>` and
`basin::solver::trf` to `basin::solver::bounded_levenberg_marquardt`. The
algorithm and generic parameters are unchanged. Use the renamed type to keep
the old method; `TrustRegionReflective` is a separate solver.

<h3 id="solver-type-annotations">Adjust explicit type annotations</h3>

`NelderMead`, `Gbnm`, `Cobyla`, `SolisWets`, and `GlobalBestPso` now include the
parameter vector type before the scalar. For example, `Cobyla::<f32>` becomes
`Cobyla::<Vec<f32>, f32>`. Inferred constructor calls usually need no annotation.
For `GaussNewton` with `f32`, supply its third parameter:
`GaussNewton<Vec<f32>, DenseMatrix<f32>, f32>`. Generic wrappers must carry
`F: Scalar` where required by the solver type.

L-BFGS now uses `Lbfgs<V, F, Mode, L>`. Replace
`Lbfgs::<Unbounded>::new()` with `Lbfgs::new().unbounded()`, and likewise call
`.unbounded()` after `with_line_search(search)` for an unbounded custom search.
`Lbfgsb` remains the bounded alias.

<h3 id="renamed-setters">Replace abbreviated setters</h3>

The deprecated aliases below are removed. Call the replacement on the same
solver or line search.

| Component | Removed method | Replacement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Brent`, `BrentDerivative`, `GoldenSection` | `with_tol(relative, absolute)` | `new().with_relative_position_tolerance(relative).with_absolute_position_tolerance(absolute)` |
| `BrentRoot` | `with_tol(relative, absolute)` | `with_relative_position_tolerance(relative).with_absolute_position_tolerance(absolute)` |
| Gauss-Newton and both LM variants | `with_tol_grad` | `with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` |
| `BoundedLevenbergMarquardt` (formerly `Trf`) | `with_tol_grad` | `with_absolute_scaled_gradient_tolerance` |
| Both LM variants | `with_tol_grad_rel`, `with_tol_cost_rel`, `with_tol_step_rel` | `with_gradient_orthogonality_tolerance`, `with_relative_model_reduction_tolerance`, `with_relative_step_tolerance` |
| `LevenbergMarquardtQr` | `with_rank_tolerance` | `with_relative_rank_tolerance` |
| Bounded `Lbfgs` | `with_tol_pg` | `with_absolute_projected_gradient_tolerance` |
| `Bfgs`, `Lbfgs` | `with_epsilon` | `with_relative_curvature_tolerance` |
| `Gbnm` | `with_small_tolerance`, `with_flat_tolerance` | `with_normalized_simplex_size_tolerance`, `with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance` |
| `Gbnm` | `with_degeneracy_tolerances(edge, determinant)` | `with_edge_ratio_tolerance(edge).with_normalized_determinant_tolerance(determinant)` |
| `BarrierMethod`, `AugmentedLagrangianMethod` | `with_tol` | `with_absolute_duality_gap_tolerance`, `with_absolute_feasibility_tolerance`, respectively |
| `BarrierMethod` | `with_phase_one_tol` | `with_absolute_phase_one_gap_tolerance` |
| `Newuoa`, `Bobyqa`, `Lincoa`, `Cobyla` | `with_rho_beg`, `with_rho_end` | `with_initial_radius`, `with_final_radius` |
| `Mads` | `with_min_poll_size` | `with_minimum_poll_size` |
| `SolisWets` | `with_rho_init` | `with_initial_step_size` |
| `MaLsChCma` | `with_initial_sigma_fallback` | `with_initial_scale_fallback` |
| `Backtracking` | `c` | `with_sufficient_decrease_coefficient` |
| `Wolfe` | `c1`, `c2` | `with_sufficient_decrease_coefficient`, `with_curvature_coefficient` |
| `MoreThuente` | `ftol`, `gtol`, `xtol` | `with_sufficient_decrease_coefficient`, `with_curvature_coefficient`, `with_relative_bracket_tolerance` |
| `HagerZhang` | `delta_sigma`, `epsilon` | `with_wolfe_coefficients`, `with_relative_cost_relaxation_tolerance` |

<h3 id="inner-solver-settings">Configure barrier and augmented-Lagrangian inner solvers</h3>

`BarrierMethod::new`, `AugmentedLagrangianMethod::new`, and
`with_inner_grad_tol` are removed. Use `with_inner_solver(inner)` and configure
the inner solver's convergence before passing it in:

```rust
let solver = BarrierMethod::with_inner_solver(
    GradientDescent::with_line_search(Backtracking::new())
        .with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8),
);
```

The outer methods no longer install an implicit inner gradient test. They also
now require surrogate stationarity alongside feasibility or duality gap, which
can change when a run converges. Set
`with_absolute_stationarity_tolerance(None)` and
`with_relative_stationarity_tolerance(None)` to recover the previous
feasibility/gap-only stopping rule.

<h2 id="changed-defaults">Check changed solver defaults</h2>

<h3 id="nelder-mead-convergence-defaults">Nelder-Mead stops on simplex collapse by default</h3>

Nelder-Mead now enables both simplex size and cost-spread checks, including in
adaptive and projected modes. Both absolute tolerances default to `1e-8`; both
relative tolerances default to about `1.5e-8` for `f64` and `3.8e-6` for `f32`.
The two checks must both pass. A run that previously continued to its execution
limit may therefore stop earlier.

To retain the 1.x behavior with no simplex convergence check, disable all four
settings:

```rust
let solver = NelderMead::new()
    .with_absolute_simplex_size_tolerance(None)
    .with_relative_simplex_size_tolerance(None)
    .with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance(None)
    .with_relative_simplex_cost_tolerance(None);
```

If you previously configured only absolute tolerances, disable both relative
settings to preserve that policy. Simplex setters now return `NelderMead`
directly; remove explicit `ConfiguredSolver<..., SimplexChecks<...>>` annotations
used for those settings. The [Nelder-Mead reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nelder_mead/struct.NelderMead.html#convergence)
gives the comparison formulas and scaling details.

<h3 id="l-bfgs-b-stopping-defaults">Bounded L-BFGS changes its f64 tolerances</h3>

The default absolute projected-gradient tolerance changes from `1e-10` to
`1e-9`. A native relative-cost reduction test is also enabled at
`10 * f64::EPSILON`, so a small reduction can stop the run before the gradient
test passes. To retain the old defaults:

```rust
let solver = basin::Lbfgsb::<Vec<f64>>::new()
    .with_absolute_projected_gradient_tolerance(1e-10)
    .with_relative_cost_reduction_tolerance(None);
```

The `f32` and unbounded defaults are unchanged.

<h3 id="lm-damping-default">LM uses trust-region damping by default</h3>

<span id="lm-and-trf-stopping-defaults"></span>

`LevenbergMarquardt` and `LevenbergMarquardtQr` now default to
`LmDamping::TrustRegion` for both scalar types. To retain Nielsen damping:

```rust
let solver: basin::LevenbergMarquardt<Vec<f64>, basin::DenseMatrix> =
    basin::LevenbergMarquardt::new()
        .with_damping(basin::LmDamping::Nielsen);
```

`with_tau()` only configures Nielsen damping; it does not select it. With the
new default, use `with_initial_step_bound()` to configure the initial trust
radius.

<h2 id="extensible-results-and-diagnostics">Use constructors for public records</h2>

<span id="extensible-solver-publications"></span>
<span id="extensible-stopping-records"></span>
<span id="enum-payload-compatibility"></span>
<span id="extensible-line-search-configurations"></span>
<span id="extensible-state-shape-errors"></span>

Result types, evaluation counts, line-search configurations, and many diagnostic
records are now non-exhaustive. Replace struct literals and struct update syntax
with public constructors or builders. For example, use
`OptimizationResult::new(state, counts, report)` and
`Wolfe::new().with_curvature_coefficient(0.8)`.

For `EvalCounts`, start with `EvalCounts::default()` and assign the fields you
need. Include `..` when destructuring non-exhaustive records, and a fallback
arm when matching non-exhaustive enums. Public fields remain available for
reading and assignment.

<h2 id="checkpoint-files">Move saved runs before upgrading</h2>

A 1.x exact checkpoint cannot be resumed in 2.0. Keep the matching 1.x
application to finish the run, or load the checkpoint there, export its
parameters in an application-owned format, and start a fresh 2.0 run. The same
approach works for state files whose types or layouts changed.

<span id="state-checkpoints"></span>
<span id="exact-checkpoints"></span>

Basin 2.0 also removes the readers for legacy bincode files. State files must
use the postcard format introduced in Basin 1.15; that file format alone does
not make an old state's serialized fields compatible with its 2.0 replacement.
Exact files require matching Basin versions and concrete solver and state types.

Within 2.0, retain solver and state together for exact continuation:

```rust
let checkpoint = executor.run_with_solver()?.into_checkpoint();
let result = Executor::resume_from_checkpoint(problem, checkpoint)
    .max_iter(2_000)
    .run()?;
```

State-only annealing and PSO snapshots no longer support `Executor::resume`.
Passing progress to `Executor::new` starts a fresh run at iteration zero; exact
continuation preserves the iteration count and model history. Reattach execution
controls and observers when resuming.

<h2 id="custom-integrations">If you implement solvers or backends</h2>

Custom `Solver::next_iter` implementations now return `Result<SolverStep<S>, E>`
instead of `(S, Option<TerminationReason>)`. Use `SolverStep::completed(state)`
to continue, `stopped(state, termination)` for a partial stopping step, or
`completed_with_termination(state, termination)` for a completed stopping step.
Only completed steps increment the iteration count. `terminate` and
`check_convergence` now return `Option<Termination<S::Float>>`, and `State::Float`
must implement `Scalar`.

Replace `run_loop(..., criteria, max_iter)` with
`run_loop_with_control(problem, state, solver, &mut control)` and configure a
`RunControl`. In custom inner solvers, replace `ResumableInner::segment_criteria`
with `configure_segment(&mut self, state, control)`, and change
`MemeticInner::seed_scaled` to take `&mut self`.

Custom matrix implementations must move shape methods from `MatrixIndex` into
`MatrixShape`. Custom vector backends using BFGS must implement `DenseBackend`
for matrix inference, or select the matrix explicitly with
`Bfgs::<V, F, M, L>::with_matrix_and_line_search(search)`. Stateful custom line
searches must implement `LineSearch::reset` so fresh runs reset their history.
